{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at cdec8accf07339733c97c9f9f431343f0d4236e9. All 73 artifacts covered: eight changed artifacts read, 65 byte-identical to the exact E7 review at 6f470379; byte-credit manifest /tmp/artroom-checker-E8-byte-credit.json. The three prior findings are fixed at their reported boundaries: reservations retain the installed owner, schema-2 marked installations are cleaned up without touching successor credentials, and live process ownership replaces age-based lock takeover. Two new P2 recovery gaps remain.\n\n1. Keep every unresolved installation's evidence until its credential is proved replaced or removed (packages/cli/src/main.ts:691). The single installing slot is overwritten by each subsequent installer. If Y writes its credential but its config mapping fails before being saved, owner.installing is its only cleanup evidence. Z then saves installing:Z, and a failure before Z writes its credential drops Y's sole evidence while Y's credential is still present. Release Y searches the old installed party, installing:Z and an absent mapping, so its installation ID is absent from the recorded intent and nothing is removed or reported as a manual duty. My real-CLI/real-git diagnostic installs X, makes the config unavailable during Y's installation (after the installing record; its credential is written before the mapping fails), restores the exact previous config, then interrupts Z at workspace-installing. Release Y returns success and leaves Y's unchanged credential file; the owner now names X and Z, and Y has no mapping. This models a local failure at the credential/config boundary, not a production deployment. Preserve multiple pending installation records, or reconcile the credential's marker against the previous installing party under the destination lock before replacing that slot. Never discard the previous party solely because a new intent is recorded. Add this two-interruption case, across Rooms as well as lanes, and retain successor-file protection.\n\n2. Bound waiting for an occupied recovery lock (packages/cli/src/git.ts:210-217,232-236). With a provably dead owner.lock holder, recover() returns when owner.lock.break is occupied by a live process, another host, or an incomplete/unreadable record. locked() immediately continues the loop, bypassing both the deadline test and sleep. It spins forever instead of timing out or naming the unresolved recovery file. An empty break file is a legitimate crash window between exclusive creation and writing its holder. Three separate-process probes set lockWait.ms=50 and each was still running with no result after 500 ms; I killed only those probe processes. Both lock files remain unchanged. Apply the bounded deadline/backoff to every unsuccessful acquisition/recovery path; identify a foreign or incomplete recovery file for manual action when it cannot be proved safe. Preserve the new rule that neither age nor ambiguous ownership permits removing a lock. Add tests for each occupied-break state and a concurrently completing recoverer.\n\nArchitecture: repository-wide installed ownership, in-progress installations and reservations are the right distinct facts. Recording release installation IDs before sending is safer than comparing a mutable revision. Durable evidence must survive all interrupted local effects, including a second attempted installation. Physical git-common-dir ownership still coordinates worktrees and Rooms. Signed-act journaling continues to retain exact envelopes across transport uncertainty; neither new finding changes Room admission or main publishing authority.\n\nSecurity: credential marker/installation-ID checks retain newer credentials and conservative legacy manual cleanup. Process/host/token locking correctly protects a live holder and avoids deleting a successor lock in the covered tests. The remaining orphan-credential duty needs repair before approving this revision. Real Room service-binding authentication, bearer MCP service wiring and deployed token rotation are still separate integration work; these client/CLI tests mostly use FakeRoom, so their success does not prove deployed authority.\n\nSimplification: retain one destination record and one bounded locking protocol, but let unresolved installations be plural until proved settled. Keep the full E/CLI/MCP scope and served-MCP follow-up 8ae3b2dc; estimates cannot reduce it.\n\nValidation: npm ci --ignore-scripts installed 352 packages; root strict workspace type checks passed; client 86 Node + 2 workerd, CLI 89, MCP 66 Node + 1 workerd all passed. Four added diagnostics passed (one orphan-installation case, three occupied-break cases). CLI build succeeded, 953.1 KiB, and built bin --help passed. Four npm packs inspected (contract/client/CLI/MCP), including CLI launcher and bundled code. MCP Worker helper bundles locally with node compatibility externals; it is not a standalone deployed route. Commands for nonexistent client/MCP build scripts and for a config-free Wrangler deploy were checker invocation errors, not candidate failures; no deployment occurred. Saved diagnostics: /tmp/artroom-checker-E8-diagnostics/checker-v8-review.test.ts. Temporary test removed; isolated candidate and main working trees clean.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ecc906f43807701c6e1f3498cc70bd1f42a2239f","binding":"assigned","head":"cdec8accf07339733c97c9f9f431343f0d4236e9","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46798c1bc964e03132f837294ce3fc71ca21e534\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3f934a98fa8efcc34c1695fee9b59e58568876e3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46798c1bc964e03132f837294ce3fc71ca21e534","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}